    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-distance-along-a-great-circle">
        <title>Distance Along a Great Circle</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the nautical mile; <m>1^\circ</m> of a great circle is <m>60</m> nautical miles, or <m>111.2\,\text{km}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find distances along a meridian and along the equator in nautical miles and in kilometres, going the shorter way round; find a new latitude after sailing due north or due south.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find distances between places in East Africa.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners turn an angle at the centre of the earth into a distance, in kilometres by the arc-length rule and in nautical miles by counting minutes, and compare the two.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Remind learners of the length of an arc, <m>\frac{\theta}{360} \times 2\pi r</m>, from Grade 11. Give <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m> and <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m> (<xref ref="note-earth-constants"/>).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-how-wide-is-kenya-at-the-equator">
            <title>How Wide Is Kenya at the Equator?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The equator enters Kenya from Uganda at about <m>34^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and leaves it into Somalia at about <m>41^\circ\,\text{E}</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Through what angle of longitude does the equator cross Kenya?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>41^\circ - 34^\circ = 7^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The equator is a great circle of radius <m>6370\,\text{km}</m>. Find its length, then the length of an arc of <m>7^\circ</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2 \times \tfrac{22}{7} \times 6370 = 40\,040\,\text{km}</m>; <m>\tfrac{7}{360} \times 40\,040 = 778.6\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Sailors and pilots measure distance in minutes of arc. How many minutes are there in <m>7^\circ</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>7 \times 60 = 420'</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>An arc of one minute on a great circle is called a nautical mile, and is about <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m>. Change <m>420</m> nautical miles to kilometres and compare with your answer to (b).</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>420 \times 1.853 = 778.3\,\text{km}</m>. The small difference from <m>778.6\,\text{km}</m> comes from rounding the nautical mile to <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define the nautical mile and state both ways of finding a distance along a great circle.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (b) and (d) are the two routes to the same distance: arc length in kilometres, or minutes of arc in nautical miles.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Nautical miles count minutes of arc on a great circle.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Meridians and the equator are great circles: along a meridian use the difference in latitude, along the equator the difference in longitude.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Along the equator, go the shorter way round.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-distance-along-a-great-circle">
            <title>Distance Along a Great Circle</title>
            <p>
                A <term>nautical mile</term> (nm) is the length of an arc of a great circle that subtends one minute (<m>1'</m>) at the centre of the earth. So along a great circle the distance in nautical miles is the angle at the centre in minutes, and <m>1^\circ = 60\,\text{nm}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                For an angle <m>\theta^\circ</m> at the centre, the distance along a great circle is
                <md>\text{distance} = 60\theta\,\text{nm} \qquad\text{or}\qquad \text{distance} = \frac{\theta}{360} \times 2\pi R\,\text{km}</md>.
                With <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m> and <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m>, <m>1^\circ = 60\,\text{nm} = 111.2\,\text{km}</m> and <m>1\,\text{nm} = 40\,040 \div 21\,600 = 1.8537\ldots\,\text{km}</m>, which this chapter takes as <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Along a meridian, <m>\theta</m> is the difference in latitude. Along the equator, <m>\theta</m> is the difference in longitude, taking the smaller of the two ways round.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <note xml:id="note-international-nautical-mile">
            <title>The International Nautical Mile</title>
            <p>
                Since 1929 the international nautical mile has been defined as exactly <m>1.852\,\text{km}</m>. With the values of this chapter we get <m>1.8537\,\text{km}</m>, which we use as <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m>. That agrees with <m>1.852\,\text{km}</m> to about <m>1</m> part in <m>2000</m>, so either gives the same answers to the accuracy we need.
            </p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-garissa-to-mombasa">
            <title>Garissa to Mombasa</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Garissa <m>(0^\circ 27'\,\text{S})</m> and Mombasa <m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{S})</m> to be on the same meridian, <m>39^\circ 40'\,\text{E}</m>. Find the distance between them along the meridian in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Both are south of the equator, so subtract: <m>4^\circ 03' - 0^\circ 27' = 3^\circ 63' - 0^\circ 27' = 3^\circ 36'</m>. In minutes, <m>3 \times 60 + 36 = 216'</m>, so the distance is <m>216\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
                <p>In kilometres, <m>216 \times 1.853 = 400.2\,\text{km}</m>. By arc length, <m>3^\circ 36' = 3.6^\circ</m> and <m>\tfrac{3.6}{360} \times 40\,040 = 400.4\,\text{km}</m>. Both are fine: the first uses the rounded value <m>1.853</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-across-the-equator">
            <title>Across the Equator</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A survey aircraft flies along the meridian <m>38^\circ 45'\,\text{E}</m> from Addis Ababa <m>(9^\circ 02'\,\text{N})</m> to latitude <m>6^\circ 48'\,\text{S}</m>. How far does it fly, in nautical miles and in kilometres?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The equator lies between the two points, so add: <m>9^\circ 02' + 6^\circ 48' = 15^\circ 50' = 950'</m>. The distance is <m>950\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
                <p>In kilometres, <m>15^\circ 50' = 15\tfrac{5}{6}^\circ</m> and <m>15\tfrac{5}{6} \div 360 \times 40\,040 = 1761.0\,\text{km}</m> (or <m>950 \times 1.853 = 1760.4\,\text{km}</m>).</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-along-the-equator-short-way">
            <title>Along the Equator, the Short Way</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Two points on the equator are at <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and <m>150^\circ\,\text{W}</m>. Find the shorter distance between them along the equator.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The longitudes are on opposite sides of Greenwich, so add: <m>37^\circ + 150^\circ = 187^\circ</m>. This is more than <m>180^\circ</m>, so the other way round, across the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian, is shorter: <m>360^\circ - 187^\circ = 173^\circ</m>.</p>
                <p>Distance <m>= 173 \times 60 = 10\,380\,\text{nm}</m>, or <m>\tfrac{173}{360} \times 40\,040 = 19\,241.4\,\text{km}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-sailing-due-south">
            <title>Sailing Due South</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A ship leaves Mombasa <m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m> and sails <m>300\,\text{nm}</m> due south. Find its new position.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Sailing due south keeps the ship on its meridian. <m>300\,\text{nm}</m> is <m>300' = 5^\circ</m> of latitude, and the ship moves further from the equator: <m>4^\circ 03' + 5^\circ = 9^\circ 03'</m>. The new position is <m>(9^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list. Take <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m> and <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m>. Distances in km within 0.1% of the exact value are accepted.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-distance-along-a-meridian">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-distance-along-a-meridian" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/02-distances-on-the-earths-surface/Distance-along-a-meridian.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-distance-along-the-equator">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-distance-along-the-equator" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/02-distances-on-the-earths-surface/Distance-along-the-equator.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-places-a-given-distance-apart">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-two-places-a-given-distance-apart" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/02-distances-on-the-earths-surface/Two-places-a-given-distance-apart.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-distance-along-a-great-circle">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-meridian-distance-same-hemisphere">
                <statement><p>P is at <m>(12^\circ\,\text{N},\ 30^\circ\,\text{E})</m> and Q is at <m>(25^\circ\,\text{N},\ 30^\circ\,\text{E})</m>. Find the distance PQ along the meridian in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>780\,\text{nm}</m>; <m>1445.9\,\text{km}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Difference in latitude <m>25^\circ - 12^\circ = 13^\circ = 780'</m>, so <m>780\,\text{nm}</m>. <m>\tfrac{13}{360} \times 40\,040 = 1445.9\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-meridian-distance-across-equator">
                <statement><p>P is at <m>(8^\circ 20'\,\text{N},\ 45^\circ\,\text{E})</m> and Q is at <m>(14^\circ 50'\,\text{S},\ 45^\circ\,\text{E})</m>. Find PQ along the meridian in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>1390\,\text{nm}</m>; <m>2576.6\,\text{km}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Opposite sides of the equator: <m>8^\circ 20' + 14^\circ 50' = 22^\circ 70' = 23^\circ 10' = 1390'</m>, so <m>1390\,\text{nm}</m>. <m>23\tfrac{1}{6} \div 360 \times 40\,040 = 2576.6\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-equator-distance">
                <statement><p>Two points on the equator are at <m>10^\circ\,\text{W}</m> and <m>65^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. Find the distance between them along the equator in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>4500\,\text{nm}</m>; <m>8341.7\,\text{km}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Opposite sides of Greenwich: <m>10^\circ + 65^\circ = 75^\circ = 4500'</m>, so <m>4500\,\text{nm}</m>. <m>\tfrac{75}{360} \times 40\,040 = 8341.7\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-equator-shorter-way">
                <statement><p>Two points on the equator are at <m>120^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and <m>100^\circ\,\text{W}</m>. Find the shorter distance between them along the equator in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>8400\,\text{nm}</m>; <m>15\,571.1\,\text{km}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>120^\circ + 100^\circ = 220^\circ</m>, more than <m>180^\circ</m>, so the shorter arc is <m>360^\circ - 220^\circ = 140^\circ = 8400'</m>: <m>8400\,\text{nm}</m>. <m>\tfrac{140}{360} \times 40\,040 = 15\,571.1\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-dhow-sails-north-from-lamu">
                <statement><p>A dhow leaves Lamu <m>(2^\circ 16'\,\text{S},\ 40^\circ 54'\,\text{E})</m> and sails <m>450\,\text{nm}</m> due north. Find its new position.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(5^\circ 14'\,\text{N},\ 40^\circ 54'\,\text{E})</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>450' = 7^\circ 30'</m> of latitude northwards. The first <m>2^\circ 16'</m> take the dhow to the equator, and the remaining <m>7^\circ 30' - 2^\circ 16' = 5^\circ 14'</m> take it north of it. The new position is <m>(5^\circ 14'\,\text{N},\ 40^\circ 54'\,\text{E})</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-latitude-from-distance">
                <title>Challenge: Latitude from a Distance</title>
                <statement><p>A research ship leaves Mogadishu, taken to be at <m>(2^\circ 03'\,\text{N},\ 45^\circ 19'\,\text{E})</m>, and sails <m>900\,\text{km}</m> due south. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.</p></statement>
                <hint><p><m>900\,\text{km}</m> is <m>900 \div 40\,040</m> of a full turn round a great circle.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>(6^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 45^\circ 19'\,\text{E})</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The ship moves along its meridian through <m>\theta = \tfrac{900}{40\,040} \times 360 = 8.092^\circ</m>, and <m>0.092 \times 60 = 5.5</m>, so <m>8^\circ 06'</m> to the nearest minute. The first <m>2^\circ 03'</m> take it to the equator; the remaining <m>8^\circ 06' - 2^\circ 03' = 6^\circ 03'</m> take it south of the equator. New position <m>(6^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 45^\circ 19'\,\text{E})</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-distance-along-a-great-circle">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>The activity and <xref ref="example-garissa-to-mombasa"/>. Give a card: <m>1^\circ = 60\,\text{nm} = 111.2\,\text{km}</m>. Assign <xref ref="ex-meridian-distance-same-hemisphere"/> and <xref ref="ex-equator-distance"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-meridian-distance-same-hemisphere"/> to <xref ref="ex-dhow-sails-north-from-lamu"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-latitude-from-distance"/>. Extension: how far is it from the North Pole to the equator in nautical miles? (Answer: <m>90^\circ = 5400\,\text{nm}</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-distance-along-a-parallel">
        <title>Distance Along a Parallel of Latitude</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a parallel of latitude <m>\phi</m> is a small circle of radius <m>R\cos\phi</m>; <m>1^\circ</m> of longitude along it is <m>60\cos\phi</m> nautical miles.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Know (Core):</alert> why the radius of a parallel is <m>R\cos\phi</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find distances along a parallel in nautical miles and in kilometres; compare the route along a parallel with the route over a pole.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> routes across the Indian Ocean and in the far north and south.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners see the parallels shrink towards the pole and find the factor <m>\cos\phi</m>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Project the interactive in <xref ref="fig-parallel-radius-cross-section"/>. Groups fill a table for the latitudes <m>0^\circ</m>, <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m> and <m>75^\circ</m>.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-shrinking-parallels">
            <title>Shrinking Parallels</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-parallel-radius-cross-section"/>. The slider sets the latitude <m>\phi</m> of the point P.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Set <m>\phi</m> to <m>0^\circ</m>, <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m> and <m>75^\circ</m> in turn. Record the radius <m>r</m> of the parallel through P each time.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>6370</m>, <m>5517</m>, <m>3185</m> and <m>1649\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Divide each radius by <m>6370</m>. What do you notice?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>1</m>, <m>0.866</m>, <m>0.5</m> and <m>0.259</m>: these are <m>\cos 0^\circ</m>, <m>\cos 30^\circ</m>, <m>\cos 60^\circ</m> and <m>\cos 75^\circ</m>. So <m>r = R\cos\phi</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the length of <m>1^\circ</m> of longitude along each parallel.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\tfrac{1}{360} \times 2\pi r</m>: <m>111.2</m>, <m>96.3</m>, <m>55.6</m> and <m>28.8\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>At what latitude is <m>1^\circ</m> of longitude half as long as it is at the equator?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>At <m>60^\circ</m> (north or south), since <m>\cos 60^\circ = \tfrac{1}{2}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To state the radius of a parallel and the distance along it.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the ratios in part (b) are <m>\cos\phi</m>, so the radius of a parallel is <m>R\cos\phi</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Along a parallel, take the great-circle distance for the same angle of longitude and multiply by <m>\cos(\text{latitude})</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A parallel is not the shortest route between two of its points, unless it is the equator.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-distance-along-a-parallel">
            <title>Distance Along a Parallel</title>
            <p>
                The parallel of latitude <m>\phi</m> is a small circle of radius
                <md>r = R\cos\phi</md>
                (<xref ref="fig-radius-of-a-parallel"/>). It shrinks to a point at the pole.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two points on this parallel whose difference in longitude is <m>\theta^\circ</m> are the same angle apart as on the equator, but on a smaller circle, so multiply by <m>\cos\phi</m>:
                <md>\text{distance} = \frac{\theta}{360} \times 2\pi R\cos\phi\,\text{km} \qquad\text{or}\qquad \text{distance} = 60\theta\cos\phi\,\text{nm}</md>.
                In the second form, <m>60\theta</m> is the difference in longitude in minutes.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-radius-of-a-parallel">
            <caption>A cross-section of the earth through the poles. P is on the parallel of latitude <m>\phi</m>, whose centre C is on the axis.</caption>
            <image width="55%">
                <shortdescription>A circle cut through the North and South Poles, with centre O, the axis and the equator. P is on the circle at latitude phi above the equator. C is on the axis level with P. OP = R, CP = R cos phi, the angle at O between the equator and OP is phi, and the angle OPC is also phi.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
% SET: earth radius R drawn as 3 units, latitude phi = 40 degrees
\def\R{3}
\def\ph{40}
% CALCULATE: P on the circle at angle phi above the equator; C on the axis level with P
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at ({\R*cos(\ph)},{\R*sin(\ph)});
\coordinate (C) at (0,{\R*sin(\ph)});
\coordinate (E) at (\R,0);
% DRAW: the meridian cross-section, the axis, the equator and the triangle OCP
\draw[thick] (O) circle (\R);
\draw[dashed] (0,{-\R-0.4}) -- (0,{\R+0.4});
\draw (-\R,0) -- (\R,0);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (P);
\draw[very thick] (C) -- (P);
% MARK: right angle at C, angle phi at O and at P
\draw (0.25,{\R*sin(\ph)}) -- (0.25,{\R*sin(\ph)-0.25}) -- (0,{\R*sin(\ph)-0.25});
\draw (0.8,0) arc (0:\ph:0.8);
\draw ($(P)+({-0.7},0)$) arc (180:{180+\ph}:0.7);
% LABEL
\node at ({1.1*cos(\ph/2)},{1.1*sin(\ph/2)}) {$\phi$};
\node at ($(P)+({-0.95*cos(\ph/2)},{-0.95*sin(\ph/2)})$) {$\phi$};
\node[below left] at (O) {O};
\node[right] at (P) {P};
\node[left] at (C) {C};
\node[above] at (0,{\R+0.4}) {N};
\node[below] at (0,{-\R-0.4}) {S};
\node[below] at ({-0.6*\R},0) {equator};
\node[above] at ({\R*cos(\ph)/2},{\R*sin(\ph)}) {$r = R\cos\phi$};
\node[below right] at ({0.5*\R*cos(\ph)},{0.5*\R*sin(\ph)}) {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-parallel-radius-cross-section">
            <caption>The radius of the parallel through P is <m>r = R\cos\phi</m>. Move the slider to change the latitude <m>\phi</m>.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-parallel-radius-cross-section" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/parallel-radius-cross-section.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-parallel-radius-cross-section" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Move the <alert><m>\phi</m></alert> slider from <m>0^\circ</m> to <m>90^\circ</m>. The thick segment CP is the radius <m>r</m> of the parallel; the dashed ellipse shows the parallel seen from the side.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Read <m>r</m> and the length of <m>1^\circ</m> of longitude at <m>0^\circ</m>, <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m> and <m>75^\circ</m>. At the pole, <m>\phi = 90^\circ</m>, the parallel is a single point.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-why-the-radius-is-r-cos-phi-core" component="core">
            <title>Why the Radius Is <m>R\cos\phi</m></title>
            <statement>
                <p>Show that the radius of the parallel of latitude <m>\phi</m> is <m>R\cos\phi</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>In the cross-section of <xref ref="fig-radius-of-a-parallel"/>, O is the centre of the earth, P is a point on the parallel and C is the centre of the parallel, on the axis. CP is parallel to the equator and perpendicular to the axis, so triangle OCP has a right angle at C.</p>
                <p>The latitude <m>\phi</m> is the angle between OP and the equator. Since CP is parallel to the equator, angle <m>OPC = \phi</m> (alternate angles). In the right-angled triangle, <m>OP = R</m> is the hypotenuse and CP is adjacent to the angle <m>\phi</m> at P, so
                    <md>CP = R\cos\phi</md>.
                    CP is the radius of the parallel.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-using-the-radius-of-a-parallel-essential" component="essential">
            <title>Using the Radius of a Parallel</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the radius of the parallel of latitude <m>45^\circ\,\text{S}</m> and the length of the whole parallel.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>From <xref ref="fig-radius-of-a-parallel"/>, the radius is <m>r = R\cos\phi = 6370 \cos 45^\circ = 4504\,\text{km}</m>.</p>
                <p>The length of the parallel is <m>2\pi r = 2 \times \tfrac{22}{7} \times 4504 = 28\,311\,\text{km}</m>. (Working without rounding the radius, <m>40\,040 \cos 45^\circ = 28\,313\,\text{km}</m>.)</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-mombasa-to-the-seychelles">
            <title>Mombasa to the Seychelles</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Mombasa <m>(39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m> and Victoria in the Seychelles <m>(55^\circ 27'\,\text{E})</m> both to be on the parallel <m>4^\circ\,\text{S}</m>. Find the distance between them along the parallel in nautical miles and in kilometres.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Difference in longitude: <m>55^\circ 27' - 39^\circ 40' = 54^\circ 87' - 39^\circ 40' = 15^\circ 47' = 947'</m>.</p>
                <p>In nautical miles: <m>947 \cos 4^\circ = 944.7\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
                <p>In kilometres: <m>15\tfrac{47}{60} \div 360 \times 40\,040 \times \cos 4^\circ = 1751.2\,\text{km}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-research-ship-southern-ocean">
            <title>A Research Ship in the Southern Ocean</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A research ship sails along the parallel <m>45^\circ\,\text{S}</m> from <m>30^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to <m>75^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. How far does it sail?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Difference in longitude <m>45^\circ = 2700'</m>. Distance <m>= 2700 \cos 45^\circ = 1909.2\,\text{nm}</m>, or <m>\tfrac{45}{360} \times 40\,040 \times \cos 45^\circ = 3539.1\,\text{km}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-over-the-pole-or-along-the-parallel">
            <title>Over the Pole or Along the Parallel?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>P is at <m>(60^\circ\,\text{N},\ 40^\circ\,\text{E})</m> and Q at <m>(60^\circ\,\text{N},\ 140^\circ\,\text{W})</m>. Compare the distance from P to Q along the parallel with the distance over the North Pole.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>40 + 140 = 180</m>, so the meridians of P and Q are opposite each other. Together they form a great circle that passes through the North Pole (<xref ref="fig-pole-route"/>).</p>
                <p>Along the parallel: the difference in longitude is <m>180^\circ = 10\,800'</m>, so the distance is <m>10\,800 \cos 60^\circ = 5400\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
                <p>Over the pole: P is <m>90^\circ - 60^\circ = 30^\circ</m> from the pole and so is Q, so the angle at the centre is <m>60^\circ = 3600'</m>: <m>3600\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
                <p>The route over the pole is shorter, by <m>1800\,\text{nm}</m>: it follows a great circle.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <figure xml:id="fig-pole-route">
            <caption>Two routes from P to Q on the parallel <m>60^\circ\,\text{N}</m>: along the parallel (thick dashed) and over the North Pole (thick).</caption>
